QA1.answer_pre:
In Doc 1, the model's internal state/weights are treated as an explicit finite-state computational device: a fixed-precision recurrent model with B bits of state is formalized as a deterministic finite-state controller (or selective affine SSM with binary one-hot hidden states and {0,1} transition matrices), and its internal memory directly bounds expressive power (2^B configurations, regular languages without tools). Weights/transition matrices there are constructive objects used to prove exact simulation results (e.g., realizing any finite-state controller exactly, achieving Turing completeness via tool access with O(log|Q|+log|Γ|) controller bits). The state is functional and combinatorial—it literally encodes machine states one-hot, and selectivity (observation-dependent transition matrices) is the essential architectural ingredient.

In Doc 2's prompting-complexity framework, by contrast, the model's internal state and weights are treated as a fixed, opaque black box (the pretrained LLM's parameters θ) that is not modified or formally decomposed; instead, complexity is measured externally via the shortest prompt (Kolmogorov-complexity-style prompt length) needed to make the fixed model produce a target text or behavior, so the LLM's internal state serves as a static "background" resource/oracle whose weights are never analyzed as an information-carrying finite-state machine, unlike Doc 1's explicit bit-accounting of internal states and transition matrices.

{"answer_pre":"Doc 1 formalizes the model's internal state/weights explicitly as a finite-state computational resource: a fixed-precision recurrent model with B bits of internal state is modeled as a deterministic finite-state controller (or realized exactly by a one-layer selective affine SSM with binary one-hot hidden states and {0,1} transition matrices), and this internal memory directly determines expressive power (2^B states, regular languages without tools, O(log|Q|+log|Γ|) controller bits needed for Turing completeness via tool interaction). The weights (transition matrices) are constructive, exactly analyzed objects, and selectivity (observation-dependent transition matrices) is the key architectural ingredient enabling exact finite-control realization. In contrast, Doc 2's prompting-complexity framework treats the LLM's internal state and weights as a fixed, unanalyzed black box (parameters θ) that is never decomposed into bits or transition structure; instead, complexity is measured externally through the length of the shortest prompt (a Kolmogorov-complexity-style quantity) needed to elicit a target text or behavior from that fixed model. So Doc 1 makes internal state/weights the object of formal, bit-level analysis governing expressivity, while Doc 2 holds internal state/weights fixed and opaque, shifting all complexity accounting onto the external prompt.","evidence_pages":[1,2,3,5,6,10,11,12,13]}

