{"answer_pre":"MuScriptor (Doc 1) uses instrument conditioning where the model accepts a list of target instruments to be transcribed. The mel-spectrogram is projected to the latent dimension and concatenated with embeddings for the present instrument classes obtained via a learned lookup table. This embedding sequence serves as prefix conditioning for the transformer model, allowing the model to condition on which instruments are present in the full track for transcription. LEXIC (Doc 3) employs difficulty-based conditioning for reading comprehension prediction. The LEXIC-Concat variant appends three precomputed word-level difficulty signals (surprisal, word frequency, and word length) as additional channels to the per-fixation input, yielding a 7-channel input tensor. The LEXIC-Res variant uses a residual mechanism with a NormativeGazeHead that predicts typical-reader gaze based on these difficulty signals, computing the residual as the deviation from what a typical reader would produce given local difficulty. Both conditioning mechanisms inject task-relevant features to guide model predictions without requiring language model inference at deployment.","evidence_pages":[4,5,15,16]}
