{"answer_pre":"In TTHE (Doc 1), round-trip consistency ensures that test harnesses remain aligned with the evolving model by requiring that generated test cases can be traced back to their original specifications, preventing drift during test-time evolution. In BACH (Doc 3), KL divergence serves as a self-regularization mechanism that controls the concentration parameters κᵣ of the spherical recognition posterior over multiple interest heads. The KL term in BACH's ELBO (Equation 20-21) pulls the recognition posterior toward a consistent contrastive posterior, preventing the concentrations from collapsing to degenerate extremes (flat or one-hot distributions) without requiring an explicit prior. While round-trip consistency in TTHE maintains bidirectional semantic alignment between specifications and tests, KL divergence in BACH maintains distributional alignment between the variational recognition model and the data-driven contrastive posterior, both acting as stability mechanisms that prevent their respective methods from drifting toward degenerate or inconsistent states during optimization.","evidence_pages":[50,51,52]}
