{"answer_pre":"Both the timestep-dependent weight in Doc 2 and the structure-aware token construction in Doc 1 address heterogeneity in their respective domains by assigning differential importance to components that contribute unequally to the learning objective. In Doc 2, timestep weighting addresses the asymmetric contribution of different diffusion timesteps to alignment quality in diffusion RLHF: early timesteps (high noise) contribute more to alignment because they shape overall image structure and semantic content, while later timesteps (low noise) primarily refine details with diminishing impact on human preference. The method assigns higher weights to early timesteps to focus learning on the most influential denoising stages. In Doc 1, structure-aware token construction addresses heterogeneity in molecular graph representations by creating tokens that capture diverse structural motifs (functional groups, bonding patterns, ring systems) at multiple scales. This recognizes that different structural features contribute asymmetrically to material properties: some atomic neighborhoods are more chemically significant than others. By constructing tokens that encode these heterogeneous structural contexts, the model can reason about structure-property relationships with greater fidelity. Both methods share the principle of adaptive weighting or representation to account for unequal information content across the input space, improving learning efficiency and predictive accuracy by focusing model capacity where it matters most.","evidence_pages":[3,4,5,45,47,48]}
```

