{"answer_pre":"Doc 1's diffusion RLHF method uses selective timestep weighting and advantage-based replay to manage signal magnitude and diversity. It applies different weights across diffusion timesteps to focus learning on informative regions and uses advantage-based replay to prioritize high-value samples, preventing wasted computation on low-signal examples. Doc 3's AdaPrefix-GRPO manages signal magnitude through adaptive prefix length control. It treats prefix length as a closed-loop controller that tracks a target success rate around 0.5, where GRPO signal is maximized. During training, AdaPrefix-GRPO measures realized batch-mean success rates and adjusts a single global base prefix length toward the target using binary search and secant interpolation, with per-sample difficulty offsets. Prefixes are annealed to zero so the deployed model solves problems unaided. The key difference is that Doc 1 manages signal through weighted sampling and replay within a fixed training distribution, while Doc 3 dynamically adjusts task difficulty (via prefix length) to maintain an optimal signal regime throughout training, converting an uncontrolled observable into a tracked set-point with per-sample difficulty offsets.","evidence_pages":[1,3,4,5,46,47,48,49,50,51]}
```

